      "model": "gpt-6.2-sol",
      "output": "{\n  \"choice\": \"within\",\n  \"base_and_horizon\": {\n    \"text\": \"Nintendo guided to consolidated profit attributable to owners of parent of 200,000 million yen for the fiscal year ending March 31, 2021 (PDF p. 2). My May 8 base case is within guidance: 200,000 million yen minus or plus 2% of that amount, with both endpoints included.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P2\"]\n  },\n  \"causal_driver\": {\n    \"text\": \"Nintendo reported that production and shipping delays were easing, though component procurement remained a risk (PDF p. 3). Management expected production and sales to meet full-year demand and planned software to launch on schedule (PDF p. 4). My base case is that supply recovers in time to recoup enough delayed Switch sales by year-end, limiting the impact on consolidated profit attributable to owners.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P5\", \"P4\", \"P1\"]\n  },\n  \"alternative\": {\n    \"text\": \"Lower: If COVID-19 prolongs component shortages, Nintendo may be unable to build and ship enough Switch units to meet demand by March 31. Sales not recovered by year-end could push profit more than 2% below guidance (PDF pp. 3–4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P5\", \"P4\"]\n  },\n  \"revision_signal\": {\n    \"text\": \"I would revise to lower if an interim Nintendo update says persistent component shortages are holding down Switch shipments and that production is no longer expected to meet full-year demand. That would overturn the recovery assumption behind the within call (PDF pp. 3–4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P5\", \"P4\"]\n  },\n  \"historical_comparison\": {\n    \"text\": \"Nintendo’s forecast table shows the 200,000 million yen guide as 22.7% below the consolidated result for the year ended March 31, 2020. The 210,842 million yen figure elsewhere on PDF p. 2 is non-consolidated profit, not the comparable prior-year result.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P2\", \"P3\"]\n  }\n}",
